WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:12.140
88. Isadora Duncan. Isadora Duncan lived at the beginning of the last century. She

2
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:18.820
was that type of artist whose work is extremely difficult to describe or capture, a dancer.

3
00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:25.420
Yet her name and her reputation are alive and well. Isadora Duncan had something magical

4
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:30.940
about her. It probably all started with her mother. Duncan's mother taught her young

5
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:38.300
daughter to love music, dance, and theatre. Unusually, her mother also insisted that Duncan

6
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:44.580
listen to the music with her soul. Duncan began to dance without rules. She wanted to

7
00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:51.180
express the feeling of the music, not stick to old-fashioned ideas of dance. Duncan was

8
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:56.980
passionate about teaching this expression and freedom to other people. She began teaching

9
00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:02.620
when she was only five, gathering the neighborhood girls and teaching them how to move their arms

10
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:11.420
like ocean waves. Duncan believed in improvisation, not tradition. She hated unnatural movements.

11
00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:17.460
She sometimes danced without shoes. She loved all of the human form and wanted all of it

12
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:24.420
to be free and moving. Many people were tired of the rules of traditional arts, and Duncan's

13
00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:31.460
philosophy appealed to them. She gained many followers and friends. Her free, natural style

14
00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:37.620
became very popular and influential. In fact, she is seen as the mother of modern dance.

15
00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:43.780
But while she was alive, she was much more appreciated in Europe than in the United States

16
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:51.620
where she was born. So she left for Europe when she was 22 years old. Isadora Duncan changed dance

17
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:59.300
forever. She minimized sets and costumes, making audiences focus on the dancer. She designed a new

18
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:05.860
type of breathing. She developed a philosophy of dance which would lead to our modern dance. She

19
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:14.180
made dance more athletic and lifted dance back up to the status of high art. Dance was reborn through her.
